  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y={x}^{2}-x-6.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=-2,-1,0,1,2,3$ and then use the formula $y={x}^{2}-x-6$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row. \n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $0$ | $-4$ | $-6$ | $-6$ | $-4$ | $0$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245131/assets/4fbf87172434c03bbdff6967d03eae3146f016dcdbb9422e89d2d94ab7693cd5.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245131/assets/007a6abdf7787cc14e40c3d92996d5224904a656f8fa6ef42a9911ce97bc24a6.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
